{
  "info": {
    "name": "MailHog Mock API v1.0.1",
    "description": "Test collection for the mock MailHog service. Base URL defaults to http://localhost:8121. In docker-compose, the service is reachable at http://mailhog-api:8121.\n\nMailHog **captures** mail instead of delivering it, and the API shape follows from that. Addresses are structured `Path` objects -- `{Relays, Mailbox, Domain, Params}` -- because what was captured is the SMTP envelope, not a rendered header. The same messages are served two ways: `/api/v1/messages` is a bare array, `/api/v2/messages` is a paginated envelope with `total`, `count` and `start`.\n\nMIME parts are addressable individually, so a single attachment can be pulled out of a captured multipart message with `/mime/part/{n}/download`.\n\n**Jim** is MailHog's chaos monkey. He is off in the seed, and `GET /api/v2/jim` returns 404 until he is switched on -- that is how a client discovers chaos is disabled. Once enabled, his failure chances are applied to the release endpoint, seeded from the recipient so the outcome is deterministic per address: `oncall@orbit-labs.com` is rejected at `RejectRecipientChance: 0.4` while `rohit.bansal@orbit-labs.com` is let through.\n\nEight messages are captured, spanning plain text, HTML, multipart/alternative, multipart/mixed with a CSV and a PDF attachment, and a multipart/report bounce. Requests labelled '(N expected)' assert an error path.",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json"
  },
  "variable": [
    {
      "key": "baseUrl",
      "value": "http://localhost:8121"
    }
  ],
